y step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nIn the diagram, the two triangles shown have parallel bases. What is the ratio of the area of the smaller triangle to the area of the larger triangle? Express your answer as a common fraction. [asy] path p = (0,0)--dir(-60)--dir(-120)--(0,0);\ndraw(p); draw(scale(3)*p);\nlabel(\"4 cm\",dir(-60)--dir(-120),S); label(\"10 cm\",3*dir(-60)--3dir(-120),S);\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"2cd9acecaddb3fb3b548a3733d02a8f2a82ee06ed28756426eefff640c7a6803"},"seconds":5.703530550003052,"seed":168475574672521259,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to determine the ratio of the areas of the two triangles given that their bases are parallel. Let's denote the smaller triangle as \\( \\triangle ABC \\) with base \\( BC = 4 \\) cm and the larger triangle as \\( \\triangle DEF \\) with base \\( EF = 10 \\) cm. Since the triangles have parallel bases, they are similar.\n\nThe ratio of the areas of two similar triangles is the square of the ratio of their corresponding sides. Here, the corresponding sides are the bases of the triangles. So, the ratio of the bases is:\n\\[\n\\frac{BC}{EF} = \\frac{4}{10} = \\frac{2}{5}\n\\]\nThe ratio of the areas of the triangles is the square of this ratio:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{2}{5} \\right)^2 = \\frac{4}{25}\n\\]\nThus, the ratio of the area of the smaller triangle to the area of the larger triangle is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{4}{25}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{4}{25}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"geometry:311"}
